#====================================================================
# Draft knowledge entry - a Python package with C extensions cannot be
#   package_type=python-require when it needs a shared-library dependency;
#   declare package_type=library instead
# Source package: biopython/1.88 (same shape already used by cffi/2.1.1)
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-ohos-conan-python-require-cannot-depend-shared-library
trigger_tool:           conan|python|cmake|link
phase:                  configuration|installation

error_signature:        Package '.*' with type 'python-require' cannot have a '(shared|static)-library' dependency to '.*'|python-require.*cannot have a .* dependency

severity:               3
category:               python, packaging, conan, dependency, linking
match_keywords:         OHOS, conan, package_type, python-require, library, shared-library, static-library, C extension, numpy, biopython, cffi, requires, dependency graph, R36

title:                  A Python package that ships C extensions must be package_type=library, not python-require, or conan rejects its shared-library dependency

root_cause:             conan enforces a package_type compatibility rule while building the
                        dependency graph: python-require is treated as a pure-Python
                        artifact (no headers/libs, nothing linkable), so it may not
                        declare a dependency on a package whose type is shared-library or
                        static-library.  A Python package that compiles C extensions and
                        links a compiled dependency - biopython's 13 extensions against
                        numpy's C-API, cffi's _cffi_backend against libffi - is therefore
                        mis-classified as python-require: the graph resolution aborts
                        before source() even runs, with a message that reads like a
                        dependency problem rather than a package_type problem.
                        The declaration is what has to change, not the dependency: the
                        library must keep linking numpy/libffi, so it is a real library
                        artifact and must say so.

affected_scope:         Python packages adapted in this repo that (a) ship compiled C
                        extensions, and (b) require another packaged artifact that is a
                        shared/static library.  Measured: biopython 1.88 against
                        numpy/2.5.2, and the same shape in cffi 2.1.1 against
                        libffi/3.5.2 (static-library).

verification_cmd:       conan graph info . -pr:h <profile> 2>&1 | grep -i "cannot have a"

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           declare-real-package-type-and-shared-option
rec_description:        Set package_type = "library" on the recipe and add the shared
                        option (options = {"shared": [True, False]}, default_options =
                        {"shared": True}), keeping settings = os, arch, compiler,
                        build_type.  This is exactly the shape already used by
                        cffi/2.1.1 in this repo, and by psutil/lxml for the same reason.
                        Do NOT keep python-require: it is a statement that the artifact
                        is pure Python, which is false as soon as extensions are built.
                        Record the reason in the recipe docstring so the next author does
                        not 'tidy' it back to python-require.
rec_command:            # in conanfile.py:
                        #   package_type = "library"
                        #   options = {"shared": [True, False]}
                        #   default_options = {"shared": True}
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               recipe-source-layout

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      drop-the-compiled-dependency-only-if-genuinely-unused
fallback_description:   Only if measurement shows the extensions do not actually link
                        the dependency (e.g. the import is pure-Python) may the
                        requirement be dropped.  That is a genuine simplification, not a
                        workaround; if any extension links the C-API, this fallback does
                        not apply and the package_type fix is mandatory.
fallback_command:       nm -D <pkg>/lib/python3.12/site-packages/<mod>/*.so | grep -i <dep-symbol>
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          recipe-source-layout

forbid_1_pattern:       delete .* from requirements\(\).*to .*(satisfy|work around).*cannot have|remove numpy.*dependency.*python-require error
forbid_1_reason:        The dependency is genuinely linked by the compiled extensions; the
                        conan message is a package_type classification error, not a
                        missing-dependency error.  Removing the requirement leaves the
                        extensions unbuilt or unresolvable at run time.
forbid_1_consequence:   A build that either fails later with unresolved symbols, or
                        'succeeds' while shipping extensions that cannot import the very
                        library they were compiled against.

forbid_2_pattern:       keep package_type *= *"python-require".*with .*ext-modules|python-require.*despite.*C extension
forbid_2_reason:        python-require asserts a pure-Python artifact; declaring it while
                        building C extensions is a false statement that also blocks any
                        library dependency from the graph.
forbid_2_consequence:   Recurring graph-resolution failures for this package and its
                        consumers, each misdiagnosed as a dependency problem.

similar_to:             [DRAFT-multipledispatch-python-require-conan-staging, DRAFT-ohos-python-require-no-host-so, DRAFT-ohos-conan-test-package-pythonpath-overwrite-clobbers-runenv]
conflicts_with:         []
prerequisite_of:        []

notes_diff_vs_no_host_so:  DRAFT-ohos-python-require-no-host-so concerns a python-require
                        package failing to export a host .so (runtime/site-packaging
                        angle).  This entry is upstream of that: it is about the
                        package_type DECLARATION itself being wrong, so the graph never
                        even resolves.  Different failure point (graph resolution vs
                        packaging/runtime), different fix (package_type vs export).
notes_cffi_precedent:   cffi/2.1.1 already documents this in its recipe docstring: it
                        needs libffi/3.5.2, whose artifact is static-library, so it
                        declares package_type = "library" with a shared option.  That
                        precedent was recorded only as a code comment, not as a knowledge
                        entry - this draft is the generalised form.
notes_numpy_shared:     numpy/2.5.2 is a shared-library artifact in this repo
                        (options shared default True), which is what triggers the rule;
                        had it been header-only the python-require declaration would have
                        passed and the mis-classification would have gone unnoticed.
